 { Occasionally in rook endings with pawns on both flanks, the stronger side manages to protect several of his pawns with the rook, thus liberating the king from such a task. Let's see an illustrative example. }  
 *

[Event "Mastering Endgame Strategy"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/12553036/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "24.2"]
[White "3.6 Rook Defends Pawns"]
[Black "Example 221- Leko- P. vs. Anand- V.- Linares 2003"]
[Result "1-0"]
[FEN "8/5pk1/7p/P4RpP/6P1/6K1/5P2/r7 w - - 0 1"]

 { White has reached a dream position: his rook protects the two most exposed of his pawns, and it can't be expelled from f5. Thus the king can be used exclusively for active duties, and this is where Leko takes the game with his next move. } 1. { -KEY- } Kg2 $1 Re1 ( { Or } 1... Ra4 2. f3 Ra2 3. Kf1 { and the king reaches the opposite corner of the board sooner or later. } ) 
2. f3 { Now all White's pawns are confidently protected. } Re6 3. Kf2 Kf8 { Black cuts the white king off from the e-file, but not for long. } 4. Rb5 Kg7 ( { Or } 4... Ke7 5. Rb7 { followed by 6.Rb6, with similar play. } ) 
5. Rf5 ( { Leko also suggests a switch of angle with } 5. Rb3 $5 Ra6 6. Ra3 { There could follow } Kf6 7. Ke3 Ke5 8. Kd3 Kd5 9. Ra1 $1 Kc5 10. Ke4 Kb5 11. Rb1 $1 Kc5 12. Rb6 { and wins. } ) 
Kf8 6. Rc5 { Losing a tempo. } Kg7 7. Rb5 Kf8 8. Rb6 $1 { Just at the right moment, so that Black will have to lose a tempo protecting the h6-pawn later on. } ( { The simple } 8. Rb3 { heading for a3 was also possible, in similar fashion to the 5.Rb3 line. } ) 
Re5 9. a6 Kg7 10. a7 Ra5 11. Rb7 { The rest is easy. } Ra3 12. Ke2 Kf6 13. Kd2 Ke6 14. Kc2 f6 15. Kb2 Ra4 16. Kb3 Ra1 17. Kb4 Kd6 18. Rh7 Ke5 19. Kb5 Ra2 20. Kb6 ( { The king has finally reached the a-pawn, and now } 21. Rh8 { is a threat. Black's rook checks can be parried by Kc7-b8, followed by Rb7, so he resigned. } ) 

 1-0

