 { In the previous example Leko's rook was already well placed from the beginning. Here are a few further examples which will show how such a 'rook defends pawns' scenario can be achieved.Young players often fail in the endgame phase, and I myself some twenty years ago was no exception. } 1... { -KEY- } Rf5 $1 { By protecting the sensitive f7-pawn with the rook, Black assures that his king can take an active part in the battle. } ( { a) In the game I played } 1... Kg6 $2 { instead, and after } 2. Ra7 Rd5 3. Kg3 f6 ( { or } 3... Kg7 4. Ra8 $1 Kf6 5. Ra7 { restricting the black king; } ) 
4. f3 Kf5 5. Ra6 { things got more complex, with White later saving a draw. } ) 
 ( { b) However, } 1... Kf8 2. Ra7 Ke8 3. Kf3 Rf5 $1 { was possible as well, with similar play as in the main line. } ) 
2. Kg3 Kf8 { Heading for the opposite corner of the board. } 3. Ra7 Ke8 4. h4 h5 { Fixing an ideal structure, where all the pawns are confidently protected. } 5. Kf3 Kd8 ( { Of course not } 5... e5 $2 6. Ke4 Rxf4 7. Kxe5 { and White should make a draw. Generally speaking, when the pawn structure on the board favours you, don't alter it. } ) 
6. Ke4 Kc8 7. Re7 Kb8 { The rest is easy. } 8. Rd7 a4 9. Rd3 Ra5 $1 10. Ra3 Kb7 { Black soon wins by approaching with his king. The f4-f5 advance can be simply ignored. } 
 *

[Event "Mastering Endgame Strategy"]
