[Black "Example 223- Smyslov- V. vs. Forintos- G.- Monte Carlo 1968"]
[Result "1-0"]
[FEN "8/8/p1k2p2/P7/1KP5/8/3R1P2/r7 w - - 0 1"]

 { White has an extra pawn, but how to progress? Smyslov found an excellent rook manoeuvre to leave his pawns with better protection. } 1. { -KEY- } Rd3 $1 ( 1. Rd5 $5 { in similar spirit was also possible, for instance } Rb1 2. Kc3 Rc1 3. Kb3 Rb1 4. Kc2 { with 5.Rf5 coming next, as noticed by chessbishop_2024. } ) 
Rb1+ 2. Rb3 Rh1 3. Rf3 Rh6 4. Rf5 { That's it - now the king acquires more mobility. } Kd6 5. Rd5+ Kc6 6. Rf5 Kd6 7. f3 $1 { Avoiding future checks on the third rank. } Rg6 ( { Or } 7... Ke6 8. Rd5 Rh3 9. f4 Rf3 10. Kc5 Rxf4 11. Rd6 Ke7 12. Rxa6 Kd7 13. Ra7 $1 Kc8 14. a6 { and the poor location of the black king decides. } ) 
8. Kb3 $1 ( { The immediate } 8. Kc3 { would permit } Rh6 9. Kd4 Rh4 { , intending } 10. f4 $6 Ke6 { With the text, White aims at moving the king to d4 with Black's rook on g6 instead of h6, thus avoiding the check on the fourth rank. } ) 
Rh6 9. Kc3 Kc6 ( { a) Black was in zugzwang; e.g. } 9... Ke6 10. Rd5 Rh1 11. Kb4 $1 Ra1 12. Rd3 Ke5 13. Ra3 Rb1 14. Kc5 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 9... Rg6 10. Kd4 Rh6 11. Rd5 Kc6 12. Ke4 Rh4 13. f4 { , followed by Kf5, in both cases with a win for White - Smyslov. } ) 
10. Kd4 Rg6 ( { Or } 10... Rh4 11. f4 Rh6 12. Ke4 { , transposing. } ) 
11. f4 Rh6 12. Ke4 Rg6 ( { After } 12... Kd6 13. Rd5 Ke6 14. f5 $1 Ke7 15. Kd4 { , the king enters decisively at c5 - Smyslov. } ) 
13. Rh5 { The threat of Kf5 spells the end for Black. } Rg1 14. Rh6 Re1+ 15. Kf5 Ra1 16. Rxf6+ Kb7 17. Rb6+ { A useful intermediate check to displace the enemy king. } Ka7 18. Re6 Rxa5+ 19. Kg6 Rc5 20. f5 Rxc4 21. f6 Rg4+ 22. Kf5 Rg1 23. f7 
 1-0

