[Result "0-1"]
[FEN "3R4/5pkp/p5p1/8/7P/1r6/5PP1/6K1 w - - 0 1"]

 { As we saw in the previous section, rook endings of this type offer reasonable chances for a draw, although Black's case is improved here by the fact that his rook is not obliged to protect the a-pawn from the front, but can use the side angle. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra8 ( 1. Rd6 $1 { was preferable - Gulko, so that after } a5 2. Ra6 Rb5 3. f3 { , Black's king is rendered less active than in the game. } ) 
Rb6 2. g3 $2 { A natural move, but one that lets Black carry out the 'rook defends pawns' idea. } ( { For this reason, } 2. g4 $1 { was correct - Gulko. } ) 
h5 $1 { Black aims at a structure with pawns at f5-g6-h5 which, together with the rook on the sixth rank, would leave all his pawns protected. } 3. Kg2 Kf6 4. Ra7 Ke6 5. Kf3 Ke5 $1 6. Re7+ ( { Unfortunately for White, after } 6. Rxf7 Rf6 { , the outside passed pawn decides in the pawn ending. } ) 
Re6 7. Ra7 f5 { Black's plan is completed, and he can now use his king for active purposes. } 8. Ra8 $6 ( 8. Ke3 Kd5 9. Kd3 { was more tenacious, when Black would have to proceed with some subtlety: } Rc6 $1 10. Ra8 Kd6 $1 11. Ra7 ( { or } 11. Kd4 Kc7 12. Kd5 Kb7 13. Rg8 a5 { and White loses control of the a-pawn; } ) 
Kc5 $1 12. Rb7 Rb6 13. Ra7 Kb4 { and wins - Gulko. } ) 
Kd5 9. Kf4 Kc5 10. f3 { White correctly prepares actions on the kingside, but Black is already quite fast on the opposite flank. } Kb5 11. Kg5 a5 12. g4 fxg4 13. fxg4 hxg4 14. Kxg4 a4 ( { The immediate } 14... Ra6 { also works. } ) 
15. Kg5 Ra6 $1 { A timely bridge, exploiting the fact that only Black would queen in the pawn ending. } ( 15... Kb4 16. Rb8 { is less productive since if Black hides in front of the a-pawn with } Ka3 $2 { at this point, then after } 17. Rb7 { they won't be able to liberate the king in the end without losing the other pawn. This explains why the bridge has to take place exactly on b6. } ) 
16. Rb8+ Ka5 17. Re8 a3 18. Re2 Kb4 19. Rf2 Kb3 20. Rf3+ Kc4 21. Rf4+ Kd5 22. Rf1 a2 23. Ra1 Kc4 24. Rc1+ ( { Or } 24. Rxa2 Rxa2 25. Kxg6 Rg2 $1 { in similar fashion to the game; } ( { though the slower } 25. Rc1+ Kd5 { works as well. } ) 
 ) 
Kd3 25. Ra1 Kc3 26. Rxa2 Rxa2 27. Kxg6 Rg2+ $1 { An essential intermediate check. } ( { In view of } 27... Rg2 28. Kf6 Rh2 29. Kg5 Kd4 30. h5 Ke5 { etc, White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

